      "output": "BEGIN_REFILL_LONG\n\nModern computer architecture relies on a hierarchical memory system to bridge the significant speed gap between the processor’s execution units and the main system memory. At the heart of this hierarchy lies the CPU cache, a small, high-speed storage area that holds copies of frequently accessed data to minimize latency. To understand how these caches function and how multiple processors maintain a consistent view of memory, one must examine the structure of cache levels, the granularity of cache lines, and the protocols governing memory coherency.\n\nThe cache hierarchy is typically organized into three distinct levels: L1, L2, and L3. The L1 cache is the smallest and fastest, located directly on the processor core. It is usually split into two separate caches: the L1 Instruction Cache (L1i), which stores machine code instructions, and the L1 Data Cache (L1d), which stores data operands. Because the L1 cache is physically close to the execution units, it offers the lowest latency, often measured in single-digit clock cycles. However, its capacity is limited, typically ranging from 32KB to 64KB per core.\n\nThe L2 cache serves as a larger, slightly slower buffer between the L1 cache and the main memory. In modern multi-core processors, the L2 cache is often private to each core, meaning each core has its own dedicated L2 cache. This allows for higher bandwidth and lower contention compared to a shared L2. The L2 cache is larger than L1, often ranging from 256KB to 1MB per core, and has a higher latency, typically around 10-20 cycles.\n\nThe L3 cache is the largest level in the hierarchy and is usually shared among all cores in a processor package. It acts as a last-level cache (LLC) before data must be fetched from main memory (DRAM). The L3 cache can range from several megabytes to tens of megabytes, depending on the processor design. While it offers the highest capacity, it also has the highest latency among the cache levels, often exceeding 40 cycles. When a core requests data, it first checks the L1 cache. If the data is not found (a cache miss), it checks the L2 cache. If still not found, it checks the L3 cache. Only if the data is absent from all cache levels does the processor access main memory, which can take hundreds of cycles.\n\nData in caches is not stored byte-by-byte but in fixed",
